course: dictionary
lesson: 5629
title: Limestone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Limestone is a rock. It is soft, and it is usually grey or white. It is made mostly from the shells and skeletons of tiny sea creatures that lived long ago.
explain: Limestone has a simple test. Put a drop of vinegar on it and it fizzes. «Eggshell and chalk are calcium carbonate, so they fizz in vinegar too, and geologists test rocks with a drop of acid: limestone fizzes.» So if a rock fizzes with vinegar, it might be limestone.
explain: Rain is slightly acidic, so over many years it slowly dissolves limestone. «Rainwater is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves rocks such as limestone.» This slow wearing away can make caves. Limestone is also used to build things, so old towns are often built from the local stone.
fact: Limestone is a sedimentary rock, which means it is formed from layers of tiny pieces that settle and pile up.
fact: Limestone fizzes when you put vinegar, which is an acid, on it.
fact: Fossils are often found in limestone.
fact: Marble is limestone that heat and pressure have changed into a rock of tightly packed crystals.
fact: Limestone is used in buildings, cement and roads, and crushed limestone is used to make glass.
exercise: What is limestone mostly made from? | Sand and mud | Ice | *Shells and skeletons of tiny sea creatures | Melted lava
exercise: Which is the best way to test a rock for limestone? | Taste it | *Put a drop of vinegar on it and see if it fizzes | Weigh it on a scale | Hit it with a hammer
exercise: «Marble is limestone that has been changed by heat and pressure.» What is marble? | Melted glass | *Limestone changed by heat and pressure into tightly packed crystals | A soft, wet clay | A type of sand
test: What happens when you put vinegar on limestone? | It melts into water | It turns blue | Nothing at all | *It fizzes
test: Which of these is made using limestone? | Rubber balls | Wool | Paper made from rope | *Cement
exam: Where are fossils often found? | In the sky | In plastic bottles | *In limestone | In sand dunes
